1403 0 obj
<<
/ADBE_NumCol 3
/ADBE_NumRow 8
/O /Table
/Summary (Table 1\072 Table showing the seven different association strategies\056 These were taken from prior work by Roberts\055Morgan et al\056 Embodied Action\072 Using gestures with the hand or body to support a description\056 For example\054 someone says\054 \140\140It feels like this\054\047\047 while stroking the floor\056 Grasping for Another Sense\072 Drawing on a sensory modality other than the one being tested\056 For instance\054 describing a shape by saying\054 \140\140This tastes strong\056\047\047 Personal Connection\072 Relating the item to a personal story or memory\056 An example is\054 \140\140This sounds like when you\220re at Ikea and you get a receipt and that sound when it comes out\056\047\047 Sensory Features\072 Describing the item using features of the relevant sense\054 such as \140\140Sharp and smooth\056\047\047Valence\072 Using words to express positive or negative qualities\054 such as \140\140This tastes horrible\056\047\047 Vocalisation\072 Making a sound or noise instead of using words\054 for example\054 \140\140This sounds like Krrrrr and tsssss\056\047\047 Familiar Experience\072 Relating the item to a common object\054 emotion\054 or texture\054 such as \140\140This smells like a banana smoothie\056\047\047)
>>
endobj
